# God's Eye View — environment template (tracked; names and placeholders only)
#
# CONTENTS — two sections, each opened by a boxed banner:
#
#   SECTION 1: SECRETS   -->  put these in .env.local
#              credentials: never commit, print, paste or share their values
#   SECTION 2: SETTINGS  -->  put these in .env
#              non-secret configuration: safe to review and share
#
# Both files are gitignored and the app reads both (Vite's dotenv ladder;
# .env.local wins when a name is in both, and a real environment variable
# beats both). On macOS the launcher can also read keys from the Keychain
# (see README); on Linux/Windows use these files or env vars.
# Only explicitly selected browser keys are injected into the client bundle.
#
# Easiest path: don't edit anything. Run the app and paste keys into the
# in-app Provider Settings panel (the POWER UP chip, bottom-right) — it writes
# them to this checkout's .env.local (owner-only permissions) and restarts the
# dev server. Under the Pinokio launcher it writes pinokio/ENVIRONMENT instead.
# Keys you supply yourself (shell env, Keychain) are shown as configured
# externally and never touched. This file remains the reference for headless
# and self-hosted setups.
#
# NOTE ON CLIENT-EXPOSED KEYS: GOOGLE_MAPS_API_KEY, CESIUM_ION_TOKEN and
# MAPILLARY_CLIENT_TOKEN are injected into the browser bundle by design
# (they're used client-side) and WILL be visible in devtools. Restrict/scope them rather than trying to hide
# them (see SECURITY.md). All other keys below stay server-side.

# ============================================================================ #
#                                                                              #
#       SECTION 1: SECRETS   -->  put these in .env.local                      #
#                                                                              #
#       Every value in this section is a credential. People, scripts and       #
#       AI assistants: never read, print, log or commit these values.          #
#                                                                              #
# ============================================================================ #
# >>> GEV-SECRETS: put these in .env.local

# --- Client-exposed: injected into the browser bundle; restrict them ---

# Optional: direct Google Photorealistic 3D Tiles, GEV place search and Street
# View (Street View also needs the Maps JavaScript API enabled for the key).
# Without it, a Cesium ion token can still load ion-hosted Google 3D; with
# neither credential, the app starts on keyless Esri World Imagery with OSM
# available in the map tray and as the automatic provider-failure fallback.
# CLIENT-EXPOSED — restrict it (HTTP referrer + API restriction) in Google Cloud.
# Get one: https://developers.google.com/maps/documentation/tile/get-api-key
GOOGLE_MAPS_API_KEY=

# Optional: Google Photorealistic 3D Tiles through Cesium ion, Bing world
# imagery, and Cesium World Terrain. The free Community plan is for eligible
# personal/non-commercial use and has quotas; check current Cesium terms. A
# direct Google key above is still required for GEV place search.
# CLIENT-EXPOSED — use a public assets:read token with URL restrictions.
# Get one: https://ion.cesium.com/tokens
CESIUM_ION_TOKEN=

# Optional: Mapillary imagery for the Street Level layer. Free client token from
# https://www.mapillary.com/dashboard/developers (register an app, copy its
# Client Token). Client-exposed by design, like GOOGLE_MAPS_API_KEY.
MAPILLARY_CLIENT_TOKEN=

# --- Server-side only ---

# Optional: a SEPARATE Google API key for the SERVER-SIDE Google calls — Places
# (/api/google/nearby-places, /api/google/text-search) and the CCTV Street View
# fallback (#33). It never reaches the browser, so it can be restricted by
# server IP (not HTTP referrer) and scoped to just Places API + Street View
# Static API, letting the client-exposed key above stay narrowly scoped to Map
# Tiles + Geocoding. Unset, these calls fall back to GOOGLE_MAPS_API_KEY, so a
# single shared key keeps working — splitting is opt-in.
# SERVER-SIDE ONLY.
# Get one: https://console.cloud.google.com/google/maps-apis/credentials
GOOGLE_MAPS_SERVER_API_KEY=

# Optional: OpenAI Realtime voice control. API key is the default cloud auth.
# Provider Settings can instead opt voice into the signed-in local
# ChatGPT/Codex OAuth session; the browser still receives only a short-lived
# Realtime client secret. Do not prefix with VITE_.
# Get one: https://platform.openai.com/api-keys
OPENAI_API_KEY=

# Optional: Gemini Live voice, selected in the GEV MIC provider chooser.
# Server-only key. Do not prefix with VITE_. Provider Settings can save it.
# Get one: https://aistudio.google.com/apikey
GEMINI_API_KEY=

# Private AISStream key for the server-side websocket. Do not prefix with VITE_.
# Get one: https://aisstream.io/apikeys
AISSTREAM_API_KEY=

# Optional: NASA FIRMS live active fires. SERVER-SIDE ONLY (never reaches the
# browser — the client polls the /api/firms proxy). Free key:
# https://firms.modaps.eosdis.nasa.gov/api/map_key/
# Without a key the FIRMS Active Fires layer is empty ("KEY REQUIRED").
# FIRMS_MAP_KEY=

# Optional: TomTom live traffic flow (BYOK freemium — the free tier is 200,000
# Traffic Flow & Incidents tile requests per MONTH, not per day; no credit card
# required. Get a key at https://my.tomtom.com/keys). SERVER-SIDE ONLY:
# the browser fetches same-origin /api/tomtom/* and never sees the key.
# Keyless fallback: the traffic layer runs its built-in simulation (white dots,
# hardcoded per-road-class speeds) — no key required for the layer to work.
# TOMTOM_API_KEY=

# Optional: OpenSky OAuth credentials (from opensky-network.org account dashboard)
# Get one: https://opensky-network.org/my-opensky/account (create an API client)
OPENSKY_CLIENT_ID=
OPENSKY_CLIENT_SECRET=

# Launch Library 2: leave blank for standard public access (no key needed).
# Optional paid Patreon tokens raise request limits; there is no free API key.
# Get one: https://thespacedevs.com/supportus
LL2_API_TOKEN=

# Optional: GitHub token for the GhostMaps layer's repository watcher. Without
# it the watcher uses GitHub's anonymous REST limit; a token only raises that
# limit. Read-only access to public repositories is enough.
# Get one: https://github.com/settings/personal-access-tokens
# GITHUB_TOKEN=

# Optional: Transport for London app key for the TfL JamCams camera list. The
# layer works keyless; a key only raises the list endpoint's rate limit.
# Get one: https://api-portal.tfl.gov.uk/
# TFL_APP_KEY=

# Optional: the News layer's Conflicts section can show Liveuamap's mapped
# reports beside GDELT, read once every 5 minutes (paid API). Used only
# without a news server (GEV_NEWS_ORIGIN in SETTINGS). SERVER-SIDE ONLY.
# Get one: https://liveuamap.com/promo/api
# LIVEUAMAP_API_KEY=

# Optional: the News layer's Local section, the day's news from the outlets
# of the country in view (free plan of 50 points a day), read once per
# country every 30 minutes, about 1.5 points a read. Without it Local says
# "key required". World News API's terms (https://worldnewsapi.com/terms/)
# allow caching only with its prior written permission: get that before
# setting the key. Used only without a news server. SERVER-SIDE ONLY.
# Get one: https://worldnewsapi.com/console/
# WORLDNEWSAPI_KEY=

# CCTV packs that need a credential (their other settings are in the CCTV block):
# ONTARIO_511_API_KEY=                                 # free developer key: sign up at 511on.ca/my511/register, then request it at 511on.ca/developers/doc
# OTTAWA_CAMERA_CERTIFICATE=                           # City of Ottawa Traffic Camera Images API certificate (free; request it via the form on traffic.ottawa.ca 'About the data'); without it the Ottawa pack is skipped
# <<< GEV-SECRETS (end of SECTION 1: SECRETS)

# ============================================================================ #
#                                                                              #
#       SECTION 2: SETTINGS  -->  put these in .env                            #
#                                                                              #
#       Non-secret configuration. Everything in this section is safe to        #
#       review and share.                                                      #
#                                                                              #
# ============================================================================ #
# >>> GEV-SETTINGS: put these in .env

# Per-IP rate limit for the Google Places cost endpoints
# (/api/google/nearby-places + /api/google/text-search), in requests per minute.
# DEFAULTS TO 120, the value the Pinokio build already ships. Raise or lower it
# with a positive integer; 60 is a sensible tightening whenever HOST is not
# localhost. Set it to exactly 0 to run unthrottled. An unreadable value (a
# typo, a stray unit) falls back to the default rather than to unlimited, so a
# slip cannot quietly disarm the guard. A positive value below 1 counts as 1.
# NOTE: this is a per-IP, process-local guard (in-memory, resets on restart;
# over-limit requests get a sanitized 429) — it is NOT a billing cap. For hard
# spend protection set provider-side budget alerts: Google Cloud Console ->
# Billing -> Budgets & alerts (plus per-API quotas under APIs & Services ->
# Quotas).
# GEV_RATELIMIT_GOOGLE_PER_MIN=60   # uncomment to tighten from the default 120

# OpenAI voice and typed chat (key: OPENAI_API_KEY in SECRETS)
# Typed chat uses Chat Completions, independently of the voice model. It can
# only bill OPENAI_API_KEY, so GEV_PREFER_CODEX_OAUTH below turns it off.
OPENAI_CHAT_MODEL=gpt-4.1-mini
# Typed chat requests (/api/openai/chat) per IP per minute, counted apart from
# voice. Unset, it takes GEV_RATELIMIT_OPENAI_PER_MIN's cap; 0 disables it.
# Not a billing cap.
# GEV_RATELIMIT_OPENAI_CHAT_PER_MIN=30
# Optional: forbid API-key voice sessions and OpenAI typed chat. Select ChatGPT
# OAuth in Provider Settings; missing or expired Codex sign-in then fails closed.
# GEV_PREFER_CODEX_OAUTH=true
OPENAI_REALTIME_MODEL=gpt-realtime-2
# Cheaper voice tier, used when the GEV MIC panel's MINI toggle is on.
# Model ids and prices are external facts that drift — if OpenAI moves this id,
# override it here rather than editing src/voice/voiceCost.js.
OPENAI_REALTIME_MODEL_MINI=gpt-realtime-2.1-mini
OPENAI_REALTIME_VOICE=marin
OPENAI_REALTIME_REASONING_EFFORT=low
# Keep only a short conversational window. Current map state is fetched live.
OPENAI_REALTIME_CONTEXT_TOKENS=3000
OPENAI_REALTIME_CONTEXT_RETENTION=0.5
# Voice-card captions of what you said. Billed separately by OpenAI and
# counted in the session cost meter and cap; off disables them.
OPENAI_REALTIME_TRANSCRIBE_MODEL=gpt-4o-mini-transcribe
# The voice debug log (.gev-logs/realtime-conversations.jsonl, rotated at
# 32 MB, never expired) omits transcripts, typed or spoken text, and tool
# arguments/results (including serialized outputs) by default. Set to 1 to
# keep them while debugging; delete the log when you are done.
GEV_VOICE_LOG_CONTENT=
OPENAI_HUD_SUMMARY_MODEL=gpt-5-nano
# Gemini Live voice, selected in the GEV MIC provider chooser (key: GEMINI_API_KEY in SECRETS)
GEMINI_LIVE_MODEL=gemini-3.8-live
# Token starts per IP per minute; 0 disables the throttle, not provider quotas.
# GEV_RATELIMIT_GEMINI_PER_MIN=30
# Per-IP rate limit for the OpenAI cost endpoints
# (/api/realtime/token + /api/openai/hud-summary), in requests per minute.
# DEFAULTS TO 30, the value the Pinokio build also ships. That clears ordinary
# use several times over: an open tab asks for a HUD summary every 15s (4/min)
# and a voice session mints one token. Raise or lower it with a positive
# integer; set it to exactly 0 to run unthrottled. An unreadable value (a typo,
# a stray unit) falls back to the default rather than to unlimited, so a slip
# cannot quietly disarm the guard. A positive value below 1 counts as 1.
# NOTE: this is a per-IP, process-local guard (in-memory, resets on restart;
# over-limit requests get a sanitized 429) — it is NOT a billing cap. For hard
# spend protection set a provider-side budget: OpenAI platform -> Settings ->
# Limits (usage limits).
# GEV_RATELIMIT_OPENAI_PER_MIN=30   # the default; uncomment only to change it
# Optional: exact socket addresses (comma-separated, no wildcards or prefixes)
# that Provider Settings treats as this machine in addition to loopback. Leave
# unset. The Docker launcher sets it to the container's gateway at start,
# because that is where Docker delivers your own browser from; it is safe only
# while the port is published on 127.0.0.1. Read SECURITY.md before setting it
# by hand.
# GEV_KEY_SETUP_TRUSTED_PEERS=

# Optional: OpenSky auth mode (oauth | auto | anon). OpenSky retired
# username/password (basic) sign-in on 2026-03-18; "basic" now loads anonymously.
# Default: oauth — requires the OpenSky client credentials in the SECRETS section
# Set to "anon" to skip aircraft auth (rate-limited but works)
OPENSKY_AUTH_MODE=oauth

# Optional: OpenSky credentials JSON file path, used when the two values above
# are empty: the credentials.json your OpenSky account page downloads when you
# create an API client. Use an absolute path for compatibility with launchers.
# OPENSKY_CREDENTIALS_FILE=/path/to/credentials.json

# Optional: dev server config
PORT=4173
# Network binding. Default is localhost — the server is reachable ONLY from
# this machine. This is the safe default: the dev server brokers your
# configured API keys, so anyone who can reach it can spend your quota.
# LAN opt-in: set HOST=0.0.0.0 to share on your local network. WARNING: that
# exposes every key-brokering proxy to everyone on the network — use only on
# networks you trust, and consider the GEV_RATELIMIT_* throttles above.
# HOST=localhost
# HOST=0.0.0.0
# Optional: comma-separated LAN hostnames to accept, in addition to localhost
# and IP addresses, for example GEV_ALLOWED_HOSTS=globe.lan,globe.internal.
# Binding HOST=0.0.0.0 alone does NOT trust every Host header; suffix and
# wildcard entries are ignored.
# GEV_ALLOWED_HOSTS=

# Optional: Live AIS vessels layer
# Browser fetch path. Keep same-origin so the private stream key stays server-side.
VITE_AIS_LIVE_API_URL=/api/vessels
# Client render cap. The server can keep a larger AISStream cache, but drawing
# every global vessel at once can overwhelm WebGL on laptops.
VITE_AIS_LIVE_MAX_ROWS=12000
# Active label cap after screen-space clustering. Labels exist for all rendered
# ships, but only clustered winners are shown to keep text readable.
VITE_AIS_LIVE_LABEL_MAX_ROWS=900
# Optional AISStream subscription filters.
# AISSTREAM_BOUNDING_BOXES=[[[-90,-180],[90,180]]]
# AISSTREAM_MESSAGE_TYPES=PositionReport,StandardClassBPositionReport,ExtendedClassBPositionReport,ShipStaticData,StaticDataReport
# Feed watchdog: silence (ms) after which the feed is REPORTED stale; the socket
# is recycled at 2.5x that. Default 120000. The watchdog self-arms only for the
# default worldwide subscription — a narrow filter above can be legitimately
# quiet, so set this explicitly (sized for your filter) to opt back in.
# 0 disables the silence watch entirely.
# AISSTREAM_SILENCE_TIMEOUT_MS=120000

# Optional: local ADS-B decoder feeds for the Local ADS-B layer. SERVER-SIDE
# ONLY. Comma-separated band=url entries; band is 1090 or 978 (UAT). Each host
# must be loopback, a private (RFC1918) address, localhost or a *.local name;
# the path must end in aircraft.json. Invalid entries are logged and skipped.
# See docs/LOCAL-RECEIVERS.md.
# LOCAL_RECEIVER_FEEDS=1090=http://localhost:8080/data/aircraft.json,978=http://localhost:8978/data/aircraft.json

# GhostMaps layer (key: GITHUB_TOKEN in SECRETS, optional). Seconds between
# checks of the watched repository's branch head; default 15, minimum 5.
# GHOSTMAPS_POLL_SECONDS=15

# Wildlife Sightings layer (iNaturalist, keyless). Upstream requests allowed
# per UTC day, shared by observations and grid tiles; default 8000.
# INATURALIST_DAILY_BUDGET=8000

# TomTom traffic (key: TOMTOM_API_KEY in SECRETS)
# Optional soft cap on upstream tile fetches per UTC day (default 6000) — a
# configurable application safety ceiling, not a guarantee of staying within
# TomTom's free allowance (currently 200K tile requests/month:
# https://docs.tomtom.com/pricing/). 6000/day keeps a 31-day month at 186K,
# inside that allowance. Over the cap the proxy serves cached/stale tiles
# instead of hitting upstream.
# TOMTOM_DAILY_TILE_BUDGET=6000

# Optional: CCTV layer tuning (advanced). Defaults are sensible — leave unset
# unless you're customizing the camera source pack.
# CCTV_SOURCES_FILE=config/cctv_sources.austin.json   # path to a source-pack JSON
# CCTV_SOURCES_JSON=                                   # inline source-pack JSON (overrides file)
# CCTV_GROUND_HEIGHTS_FILE=                            # path to the camera ground-height sidecar (default src/data/local_data/cctv_ground_heights/cctv_ground_heights.json, written by scripts/precompute-cctv-heights.mjs)
# CCTV_AUSTIN_ROWS_URL=                                # override the Austin Open Data catalog URL
# CCTV_AUSTIN_MAX_SOURCES=250                          # cap cameras loaded from the Austin catalog
# CCTV_CALTRANS_DISTRICTS=4,7,11,3                     # Caltrans districts; empty string disables Caltrans
# CCTV_CALTRANS_MAX_SOURCES=300                        # cap cameras loaded from Caltrans
# CCTV_TFL_ENABLED=1                                   # set 0 to disable Transport for London JamCams
# CCTV_TFL_MAX_SOURCES=250                             # cap cameras loaded from TfL
# CCTV_ONTARIO_ENABLED=1                               # set 0 to disable Ontario 511 cameras
# CCTV_ONTARIO_MAX_SOURCES=1000                        # cap cameras loaded from Ontario 511
# CCTV_FINTRAFFIC_ENABLED=1                            # set 0 to drop the Fintraffic (Finland) pack
# CCTV_FINTRAFFIC_MAX_SOURCES=300                      # cap cameras loaded from the Fintraffic catalog
# CCTV_DRIVEBC_MAX_SOURCES=250                         # cap DriveBC (British Columbia) cameras, nearest Vancouver/Victoria first
# CCTV_DRIVEBC_ENABLED=1                               # 0 turns off the DriveBC camera pack
# CCTV_TXDOT_ENABLED=1                                 # set 0 to disable the TxDOT (Texas) pack
# CCTV_TXDOT_DISTRICTS=AUS,SAT                         # TxDOT district codes (25 exist); empty disables the pack
# CCTV_TXDOT_MAX_SOURCES=500                           # cap cameras loaded from the TxDOT catalogs
# CCTV_TALLINN_ENABLED=1                               # set 0 to disable the Tallinn (Estonia) intersection cameras
# CCTV_TALLINN_MAX_SOURCES=255                         # cap cameras loaded from the Tallinn catalog
# CCTV_TALLINN_SOURCES_FILE=                           # path to the Tallinn camera list (default config/cctv_sources.tallinn.json)
# CCTV_TARKTEE_ENABLED=1                               # set 0 to disable the Tarktee (Estonia) road-weather cameras
# CCTV_TARKTEE_MAX_SOURCES=179                         # cap cameras loaded from Tarktee
# CCTV_WARENDORF_ENABLED=1                             # set 0 to disable the Warendorf (Germany) municipal webcams
# CCTV_WARENDORF_SOURCES_FILE=                         # path to the Warendorf camera list (default config/cctv_sources.warendorf.json)
# CCTV_NSW_ENABLED=1                                   # set 0 to disable the Live Traffic NSW (Australia) cameras
# CCTV_NSW_MAX_SOURCES=250                             # cap cameras loaded from Live Traffic NSW
# CCTV_CALGARY_ENABLED=1                               # set 0 to disable the Open Calgary (Alberta) cameras
# CCTV_CALGARY_MAX_SOURCES=220                         # cap cameras loaded from the Open Calgary catalog
# CCTV_CALGARY_ROWS_URL=                               # override the Open Calgary catalog URL
# CCTV_CATALONIA_ENABLED=1                             # set 0 to disable the Servei Català de Trànsit cameras
# CCTV_CATALONIA_MAX_SOURCES=150                       # cap cameras loaded from Catalonia (~130 exist today)
# CCTV_ICELAND_ENABLED=1                               # set 0 to disable the Vegagerðin (Iceland) road cameras
# CCTV_ICELAND_MAX_SOURCES=250                         # cap cameras loaded from Iceland (~500 views exist today)
# CCTV_HK_ENABLED=1                                   # set 0 to disable the Hong Kong Transport Department cameras
# CCTV_HK_MAX_SOURCES=1100                            # cap cameras loaded from Hong Kong (~1,013 exist today); min 8, max 1200
# CCTV_HK_CAMERAS_URL=                                # override the DATA.GOV.HK camera locations XML URL
# CCTV_KINGCOUNTY_ENABLED=1                            # set 0 to disable the King County (Washington) road cameras
# CCTV_KINGCOUNTY_MAX_SOURCES=125                      # cap cameras loaded from the King County catalog
# CCTV_WSDOT_ENABLED=1                                 # set 0 to disable the WSDOT (Washington State) highway cameras
# CCTV_WSDOT_MAX_SOURCES=250                           # cap cameras loaded from the WSDOT catalog, nearest Seattle/Spokane first; min 8, max 1200
# CCTV_VEGVESEN_ENABLED=1                              # set 0 to disable the Statens vegvesen (Norway) road cameras
# CCTV_VEGVESEN_MAX_SOURCES=900                        # cap cameras loaded from Statens vegvesen (default loads all ~850 live; max 1000)
# CCTV_VEGVESEN_VIDEO=1                                # set 0 to show stills instead of live HLS for Statens vegvesen video cameras
# CCTV_VEGVESEN_HEIGHTS=1                              # set 0 to skip Kartverket ground heights for Statens vegvesen cameras (flat prior instead)
# CCTV_VEGVESEN_URL=                                   # override the Statens vegvesen catalog URL
# CCTV_NYC_ENABLED=                                    # off by default; set 1 only if you hold a NYC DOT data-sharing agreement (tmcdot@dot.nyc.gov) for the camera feed
# CCTV_NYC_MAX_SOURCES=250                             # cap cameras loaded from the NYC TMC catalog (nearest borough cores first)
# CCTV_NYC_CAMERAS_URL=                                # override the NYC TMC catalog URL
# CCTV_TORONTO_ENABLED=1                              # set 0 to disable the City of Toronto RESCU cameras
# CCTV_TORONTO_MAX_SOURCES=336                         # cap cameras loaded from Toronto RESCU (clamped to 8–500)
# CCTV_TORONTO_LIST_URL=                               # override the Toronto RESCU camera list URL
# CCTV_LITHUANIA_ENABLED=                              # off by default; set 1 to load Via Lietuva / Eismoinfo road cameras (photo licence unconfirmed; see DATA_SOURCES.md)
# CCTV_LITHUANIA_MAX_SOURCES=400                       # cap Lithuanian cameras; no API key required
# CCTV_FLORIDA_ENABLED=                                # off by default; set 1 to load FL511 (Florida) cameras (individual, personal use only; any other use, including a public deployment, needs FDOT's express written consent; see DATA_SOURCES.md)
# CCTV_FLORIDA_MAX_SOURCES=500                         # cap cameras loaded from the FL511 catalog (nearest regional anchors first)
# CCTV_TAIWAN_ENABLED=1                                # set 0 to skip Taiwan Freeway Bureau (MOTC) road cameras
# CCTV_TAIWAN_MAX_SOURCES=600                          # cap Taiwan freeway cameras (1-2000); no API key required
# CCTV_TAIWAN_URL=                                     # override the Freeway Bureau MOTC 2.0 CCTV.xml URL
# CCTV_HOUSTON_ENABLED=                                # off by default; set 1 to load Houston TranStar cameras only if you hold TxDOT's written permission (TranStar's Content Usage terms prohibit copying, distributing or framing its images without it; see DATA_SOURCES.md)
# CCTV_HOUSTON_MAX_SOURCES=250                         # cap cameras loaded from the Houston TranStar catalog (nearest metro cores first)
# CCTV_HOUSTON_CAMERAS_URL=                            # override the Houston TranStar catalog URL
# CCTV_A22_ENABLED=1                                   # set 0 to disable the Autostrada del Brennero (A22, Italy) webcams
# CCTV_A22_URL=                                        # override the Open Data Hub A22 webcam catalog URL
# CCTV_OTTAWA_ENABLED=1                                # set 0 to skip City of Ottawa cameras (also needs OTTAWA_CAMERA_CERTIFICATE)
# CCTV_OTTAWA_MAX_SOURCES=250                          # cap Ottawa cameras (8-500, nearest downtown first)
# CCTV_QUEBEC_ENABLED=1                                # set 0 to skip the Québec camera locations (MTQ, Montréal, scenic links)
# CCTV_QUEBEC_VIDEO_ENABLED=                           # off by default; set 1 to let viewers play Québec 511 clips in their browser after pressing Play (511 terms of use unconfirmed; see DATA_SOURCES.md)
# CCTV_WORLD_CAMERAS_ENABLED=1                         # set 0 to skip the Oregon and MLIT (Japan) still cameras
# CCTV_DELDOT_ENABLED=1                                # set 0 to disable the DelDOT (Delaware) live video cameras (see DATA_SOURCES.md)
# CCTV_DELDOT_MAX_SOURCES=300                          # cap cameras loaded from the DelDOT catalog (8-400)
# CCTV_MAX_SOURCES=7350                                # catalog-wide ceiling: 4,000 slots shared round-robin, the rest reserved for Québec and World
# CCTV_PREFER_AUSTIN=1                                 # prioritize Austin cameras in the registry
# CCTV_FORCE_AUSTIN=                                   # force live default sources when a source-pack JSON is configured

# Optional: Data Library (DATA LAYERS ▸ Browse data library). Every entry is
# fetched live from its provider; nothing is hosted here. See docs/DATA-LIBRARY.md.
# GEV_LIBRARY_ENABLE=                                  # comma-separated ids of operator opt-in entries to serve (packs use their host's own switch, e.g. CCTV_NYC_ENABLED)
# GEV_LIBRARY_DISABLE=                                 # comma-separated ids to remove from this instance entirely

# Optional: comma-separated Overpass URLs; use only instances you run or pay for.
# Also enables voice place search (osm_query, "hospitals in Kathmandu").
OVERPASS_UPSTREAMS=

# Optional: place-lookup endpoints. Lookups try bundled data first, then the
# endpoint set here. Nominatim stays off unless NOMINATIM_URL is set; Photon
# uses its public service when PHOTON_URL is unset.
# NOMINATIM_URL: search endpoint for last-resort place search and voice
# outlines (e.g. https://nominatim.example.org/search). A configured URL
# selects the endpoint explicitly. Unset, empty and invalid values disable
# Nominatim; nothing falls back to nominatim.openstreetmap.org. Use an endpoint
# you operate or are authorized to use. Answers are cached (outlines 90 days,
# places 30 days, no-results 24 h in .gev-cache/nominatim).
# Explicit public-service configuration retains per-install pacing and caps;
# these do not establish an application-wide allowance or authorize public use.
# NOMINATIM_URL=
# Public-instance requests per UTC day, outlines and search together. Not
# applied to your own NOMINATIM_URL.
# NOMINATIM_DAILY_CAP=50
# Public-instance pacing is shared through .gev-cache/nominatim/state.json. If
# the server stopped mid-request (or its disk filled), it leaves a
# state.json.dispatch.* marker and refuses public lookups ("unfinished durable
# Nominatim dispatch") for ten minutes rather than risk breaking the spacing;
# the next lookup after that removes the marker and resumes with fresh spacing.
# PHOTON_URL: keyless place search endpoint used by the browser
# (e.g. https://photon.example.org/api/). Unset uses photon.komoot.io; set it
# empty to disable. Only https endpoints are accepted (the page's
# Content-Security-Policy allows no plain-http origin); any other value disables it.
# PHOTON_URL=

# Optional: who may frame the app in embed mode (?embed=1), as CSP
# frame-ancestors sources such as "https://example.com", or * for any page.
# Unset, no page may frame it. Other pages of the app can never be framed.
# GEV_EMBED_FRAME_ANCESTORS=

# Gemini typed chat uses generateContent; voice continues to use Gemini Live.
GEMINI_CHAT_MODEL=gemini-3.5-flash-lite
# Typed chat requests (/api/gemini/chat) per IP per minute. Unset, it takes
# GEV_RATELIMIT_GEMINI_PER_MIN's cap (default 30); 0 disables it.
# GEV_RATELIMIT_GEMINI_CHAT_PER_MIN=30

# Optional keyless Radio Browser coverage: up to 8 ISO alpha-2 countries.
# Each reserves up to 40 stations within the existing 750-station directory.
# Restart after changing. Leave blank for the existing global selection.
# RADIO_PRIORITY_COUNTRIES=IN,PL

# Optional: a news server for the News layer (its /api/news/hotspots, /feed
# and /outline routes), such as https://news.example. The local server
# forwards only the layer's own GET requests there, with a 15 s timeout. Set,
# it wins over the settings below. SERVER-SIDE ONLY.
# GEV_NEWS_ORIGIN=
#
# Without a news server Core reads the News layer itself. SERVER-SIDE ONLY.
# Conflicts need no key: the last 24 h of GDELT events (its 15-minute export
# files, about 100 KB each, about 10 MB a day; violence only, one report per
# story at GDELT's place for it), titled from GDELT's GKG files (about 6 MB
# each, about 23 MB an hour: the newest 3 h are read at start, then each new
# one). Liveuamap's reports can sit beside them (LIVEUAMAP_API_KEY in
# SECRETS). Local reads World News API (WORLDNEWSAPI_KEY in SECRETS); each
# country's news is kept in memory for at most 1 hour, and the app's Data
# attribution popover credits World News API with a link.
# The most World News API reads a day (default 30, about 45 points, inside
# the free plan). After that Local says the daily limit is reached.
# Raise it on a paid plan.
# WORLDNEWSAPI_DAILY_READS=30

# Rail layer: every feed is keyless, so nothing needs setting. Packs are an
# opt-in extra for live trains (the operator's lines, stop names, next stops):
# scripts/build-rail-paths.mjs (--feed <id> ... or --all) writes path and
# schedule packs, and the server reads them from this directory
# (default .cache/rail-paths). The build reads it from its own environment,
# not from this file: export it, or pass the same folder as --out.
# RAIL_PATHS_DIR=
# <<< GEV-SETTINGS (end of SECTION 2: SETTINGS)
